  "id": "COMPOUND:1362 - PROTEIN:4299",
  "verbalization": "Aminophenazone is implicated in the targeting of Sodium/Potassium Transporting ATPase subunits through a series of intermediate compounds, all of which are targeted by Cytochrome c4. This suggests a potential pathway where Aminophenazone may interact with Cytochrome c4, which in turn targets the ATPase subunits. The evidence indicates that Aminophenazone may act as a modulator or inhibitor of the ATPase, possibly through a direct or indirect interaction with the enzyme. The presence of multiple pathways converging on the same target highlights a possible mechanism where Aminophenazone influences the activity of the ATPase via a shared intermediate, Cytochrome c4. The integration of these findings supports the hypothesis that Aminophenazone may exert its effects by interacting with the ATPase through a complex network of molecular interactions, potentially involving the cytochrome c4 as a bridge. This mechanism underscores the importance of Cytochrome c4 in mediating the interaction between Aminophenazone and the Sodium/Potassium Transporting ATPase subunits.",
